 lots of resistance  arising from that there are some  concerns about money  and whilst you might not be doubling the  number of animals you might be using  animals in different cages that could  play out  there is an increased analysis  complexity and practicalities and  there's a belief in the values  but most of these are cultural and the  misconceptions  so these forces can be weakened if  we focus our minds on that  so with that in mind what we need is an  institute level plan how can we drive  change wind in an institute and  professor john cotter  who from harvard has written numerous  man  books on this and one of the 1995 he put  forward this eight step  change process for leading change and  what i like about it is that  he talks about how all the early steps  are about leadership  and the later steps are about management  because  you have to have the leadership steps so  that you win the hearts and minds and  change that inner core of the onion  model  and instead of jumping what we normally  do is we jump to how we're going to  practically change these activities on  the ground and what works dreams are  going to change  which is the focus and the management  stages and that's not going to work  he talks about how you have to work  through the stages sequentially though  some of them will start to be running  simultaneously and he also highlights  that you have to get all the way through  to the end  or any progress you make will be lost  and people will revert back to historic  behavior  so the first steps are around creating  urgency a purning platform which the nih  is doing very well for us  that you know if you want their money  you need to embrace sex  as a biological variable and then  suggest forming a powerful coalition  to then do the latest data ships to  provide that leadership  using people all across the organization  people have influence in the animal  houses  who are key players in the therapy areas  your key place in the ethical decisions  they're the people that can drive this  change but you will need the figurehead  as well from the professional bodies or  the incidents  you need to create a vision for change  and that vision needs to be a sound bite  it needs to be something that you can  quickly understand and get your hearts  in it can't be  a four-page document that nobody's  really going to read is going to be  signed and put in online and the reason  it's got to be the soundbite is you have  to communicate the vision with high  repetition and it talks in the books he  talks about how much information we  receive in a typical two-month process  so if someone says there's a new  initiative does a talk  um does a poster initiate a presentation  that's just gonna go and that's very  interesting and it drifts away to  nothing  if we really want this to change it has  to be something that we're talking about  numerously frequently and has been  raised multiple times in this area for  it truly to have an impact  and only then when we've done those  steps can we start to think about  removing the obstacles  removing the blockers that prevent  people from doing this  so we can create short-term wins when  you create the short-term wins we're  helping to maintain the energy to keep  going forward  to help communicate the vision and to  keep everyone  and that sense of urgency going and you  can use this  to refine your vision and to refine the  communication going forward  and only for these cycles will you  anchor the change in the culture so that  it truly  embeds as a new practice  so that's a sort of a business  management  perspective on it but i also had the  wonderful opportunity of  reading a sociological exploration of  the issue because i  got to read annika gomper's master's  dissertation at cambridge university  and she interviewed nine different  scientists in the us  to ask them what did they think about  sex as a biological  variable in their research and  sociological exploration is qualitative  research looking for patterns of meaning  or things  and she identified three different  themes  and said that they were conflicting so  the three things were generalizability  avoiding complexity and practicality  she highlighted how all the scientists  completely believed that it was really  important to research to understand  variation  to understand the biological story and  the difference what is happening  requires us to explore variations so it  was really  important to consider sex as a  biological variable  but we need to avoid complexity  to make progress in science we reduce  complexity we have to  simplify all this variation that could  be influencing the story into a simple  testing space so we can um  make a decision and communicate our  finding and incrementally step forward  the science  and so there are practicality concerns  and she's not talking about the  practicality of running experiments  she's talking about the practicality of  the scientific process  because there's a mediated tension  between the generalizability of our  conclusions we want to make a general  conclusion about what we found  and avoiding complexity if we want to  make a conclusion we can't say well it  depends on this age and this sex and  this diet  because we sort of get lost amongst the  trees we're trying to convert the  question into a doable problem  so we are actively avoiding looking for  a sex difference because we want a  simple answer  we need some examples where people have  actually done this and  you know and show us a way forward and  through that  it will become much easier for us  so in conclusion sex bias is culturally  embedded in our research pipelines it is  impacting the reporting the design and  the analysis  now precision medicine is touted as the  future  and we typically focus on differences in  genetic factors environmental lifestyle  but i would argue the set considering  the sex of the patient provides an easy  win to optimize the health care  so that should be our first protocol and  researchers suggest that sex  is a significant source of variation for  both invi and in vitro  so whilst we've not before looked that  doesn't mean that it isn't playing a  role in the story  this isn't an individual science issue  but a scientific practice  issue we need as a community  to actively be discussing these issues  to support scientists to be more mindful  of sex as a biological available in our  pipelines  i'd like to acknowledge numerous people  who've worked me over the years  who have helped me along with the story  particularly the impc  and the statisticians in tel aviv  university  in israel thank you very much for  listening i hope you enjoyed the talk  you
 

